OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026", "title": "Counterexamples to weak chromatic splitting: sphere kernels and descent exponents", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "318", "family_title": "Chromatic splitting: filtrations and counterexamples", "abstract_html": "For the derived <i>p</i>-completion of the sphere, the canonical weak chromatic splitting map has no homotopy retraction at height <i>p</i> for every prime <i>p</i> ≥ 5, and at height \\(p+1\\) for every prime <i>p</i> ≥ 7. The classical sphere product \\(\\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}\\) gives a nonzero kernel class in both ranges.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The image of \\(z_p\\) in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_{p-1}X\\) is nonzero and is killed by \\( \\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p,X}). \\) Consequently \\(i_{p,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction.", "statement": "<p>**Named kernel at height \\(p\\) — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectra, with sphere \\(S=S^0_{(p)}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K(a)\\) denote Morava \\(K\\)-theory of height \\(a\\), put \\(K(0)=H\\mathbb Q\\), and define\n  \\[\n  L_r=L_{K(0)\\vee\\cdots\\vee K(r)},\\qquad\n  X=S_p^\\wedge=\\operatorname*{holim}_{j\\ge1}S/p^j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a spectrum \\(Z\\), let \\(\\eta_Z^{(n)}:Z\\to L_{K(n)}Z\\) be the localization unit, and define\n  \\[\n  i_{n,X}=L_{n-1}(\\eta_X^{(n)}):\n  L_{n-1}X\\longrightarrow L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}X.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\) be prime, and put\n  \\[\n  d_p=2p(p-1)-2,\\qquad k_p=(p-1)^2,\\qquad\n  z_p=\\beta_1^{k_p}\\in\\pi_{d_pk_p}S,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\beta_1\\in\\pi_{d_p}S\\) is the classical first beta element.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image of \\(z_p\\) in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_{p-1}X\\) is nonzero and is killed by\n\\[\n\\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p,X}).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(i_{p,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the named product\n\\[\nz_p=\\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}.\n\\]\nProposition 3 detects it under the actual unit to the \\(K(p-1)\\)-local spectrum\n\\[\nY=E_{p-1}^{hG};\n\\]\nin particular, its image in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_{p-1}X\\) is nonzero.</p><p>Proposition 10, applied to \\(x=\\beta_1\\), kills that same sphere product in\n\\[\nB_p=L_{K(p)}S\n\\qquad(p\\ge5).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 1 with \\(t=p-1\\) and \\(n=p\\). Its naturality comparison places the nonzero lower image in the kernel of the actual canonical map\n\\[\ni_{p,X}:L_{p-1}X\\to L_{p-1}L_{K(p)}X.\n\\]\nA retraction would induce a left inverse on this homotopy group, which is impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The image of \\(z_p\\) in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_pX\\) is nonzero and is killed by \\( \\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p+1,X}). \\) Consequently \\(i_{p+1,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction.", "statement": "<p>**The same kernel at height \\(p+1\\) — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge7\\) be prime and work in ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectra.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X=S_p^\\wedge,\\qquad\n  L_r=L_{K(0)\\vee\\cdots\\vee K(r)},\\qquad\n  i_{n,X}=L_{n-1}(\\eta_X^{(n)}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  d_p=2p(p-1)-2,\\qquad k_p=(p-1)^2,\\qquad\n  z_p=\\beta_1^{k_p}\\in\\pi_{d_pk_p}S.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image of \\(z_p\\) in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_pX\\) is nonzero and is killed by\n\\[\n\\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p+1,X}).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(i_{p+1,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Again use\n\\[\nz_p=\\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}.\n\\]\nProposition 3 detects this named product under a \\(K(p-1)\\)-local unit and shows that its image in \\(\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_pX\\) is nonzero.</p><p>For \\(p\\ge7\\), Proposition 10 kills the same product under\n\\[\nS\\to B_{p+1}=L_{K(p+1)}S.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 1 with \\(t=p-1\\) and \\(n=p+1\\). The nonzero lower image is killed by\n\\[\n\\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p+1,X}).\n\\]\nThus \\(i_{p+1,X}\\) cannot have a homotopy retraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The image of \\( \\beta_1^{16}\\in\\pi_{608}S \\) in \\(\\pi_{608}L_4X\\) is nonzero and belongs to the kernel of \\( \\pi_{608}(i_{5,X}). \\)", "statement": "<p>**The prime-five class — original Corollary 1.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work at \\(p=5\\), with \\(X=S_5^\\wedge\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta_1\\in\\pi_{38}S\\) be the classical first beta element.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  i_{5,X}=L_4(\\eta_X^{(5)}):L_4X\\longrightarrow L_4L_{K(5)}X.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image of\n\\[\n\\beta_1^{16}\\in\\pi_{608}S\n\\]\nin \\(\\pi_{608}L_4X\\) is nonzero and belongs to the kernel of\n\\[\n\\pi_{608}(i_{5,X}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Specialize Theorem 1 to \\(p=5\\). Then\n\\[\nd_5=2\\cdot5\\cdot4-2=38,\\qquad\nk_5=4^2=16,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nd_5k_5=608.\n\\]\nThe image of \\(\\beta_1^{16}\\) is nonzero in \\(\\pi_{608}L_4X\\) and is killed by \\(\\pi_{608}(i_{5,X})\\).</p><p>In the upper filtration, the class lies in\n\\[\nF^{32}\\pi_{608}B_5,\n\\]\nwhereas the zero tail begins at \\(F^{27}\\), as in the original proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\exp_H(E_{p-1})>m,\\qquad \\exp_H\\bigl(T(B_p)\\bigr)\\le m. \\) A homotopy retraction of \\( i_{p,X}:L_{p-1}X\\longrightarrow L_{p-1}L_{K(p)}X, \\qquad X=S_p^\\wedge, \\) would make \\(E_{p-1}\\) an \\(H\\)-equivariant retract of …", "statement": "<p>**Incompatible descent exponents — original Theorem 1.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\) be prime, put \\(t=p-1\\), and let \\(E_t\\) be Morava \\(E\\)-theory for the height-\\(t\\) Honda formal group over \\(\\mathbb F_{p^t}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  H=C_p\\rtimes C_{(p-1)^2}\n  \\]\n  be the finite pure-stabilizer subgroup acting coherently on \\(E_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Work in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_H=\\operatorname{Fun}(BH,\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)})\n  \\]\n  with pointwise ordinary smash product. Put\n  \\[\n  A_H=F(H_+,S),\\qquad\n  J_H=\\operatorname{fib}(S\\to A_H),\n  \\]\n  with translation action on \\(A_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(V\\in\\mathcal D_H\\), define \\(\\exp_H(V)\\) to be the least integer \\(b\\ge1\\) for which\n  \\[\n  J_H^{\\wedge b}\\wedge V\\longrightarrow V\n  \\]\n  is null, and define it to be infinity if no such integer exists.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B_p=L_{K(p)}S,\\qquad\n  T(V)=L_{K(t)}(E_t\\wedge V),\n  \\]\n  with \\(H\\) acting on the first factor, and let \\(m=p^2+2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\exp_H(E_{p-1})&gt;m,\\qquad\n\\exp_H\\bigl(T(B_p)\\bigr)\\le m.\n\\]\nA homotopy retraction of\n\\[\ni_{p,X}:L_{p-1}X\\longrightarrow L_{p-1}L_{K(p)}X,\n\\qquad X=S_p^\\wedge,\n\\]\nwould make \\(E_{p-1}\\) an \\(H\\)-equivariant retract of \\(T(B_p)\\). Hence \\(i_{p,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction, independently of the named-kernel argument.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 14 gives\n\\[\n\\exp_H(E_{p-1})&gt;m,\n\\qquad m=p^2+2.\n\\]\nProposition 17 gives\n\\[\n\\exp_H(T(B_p))\\le m.\n\\]\nThese are bounds in the same category \\(\\mathcal D_H\\).</p><p>If the canonical map \\(i_{p,X}\\) had a homotopy retraction, Proposition 17 would make \\(E_{p-1}\\) an \\(H\\)-equivariant retract of \\(T(B_p)\\). Retract invariance from Proposition 12 would then imply\n\\[\n\\exp_H(E_{p-1})\\le m,\n\\]\ncontradicting Proposition 14.</p><p>This also proves the nonretraction assertion stated separately as original Corollary 11.2. The lower bound uses finite-page survival only; the upper bound uses an actual null map and its finite layers. No named sphere representative or permanent cycle is needed for this independent argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\(c\\) is a \\(K(a)\\)-equivalence for every integer \\(a\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Completion and the canonical kernel — original Lemma 2.1 and Proposition 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectra.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  c:S\\longrightarrow X=S_p^\\wedge\n  \\]\n  be the completion map, and let \\(L_r=L_{K(0)\\vee\\cdots\\vee K(r)}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The map \\(c\\) is a \\(K(a)\\)-equivalence for every integer \\(a\\ge1\\). If \\(1\\le t\\le r\\) and \\(Y\\) is \\(K(t)\\)-local, every map \\(S\\to Y\\) factors through\n   \\[\n   S\\longrightarrow X\\longrightarrow L_rX.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Let \\(n\\ge2\\), \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\), and \\(z\\in\\pi_qS\\). Suppose a map \\(S\\to Y\\) to a \\(K(t)\\)-local spectrum carries \\(z\\) to a nonzero element, while the unit\n   \\[\n   S\\longrightarrow L_{K(n)}S\n   \\]\n   carries \\(z\\) to zero. Then the image\n   \\[\n   \\bar z\\in\\pi_qL_{n-1}X\n   \\]\n   is nonzero and satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\pi_q(i_{n,X})(\\bar z)=0.\n   \\]\n   In particular, \\(i_{n,X}\\) has no homotopy retraction.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first assertion is Lemma 1.1.</p><p>For the second assertion, Lemma 1.1 factors the detecting map through \\(L_{n-1}X\\), so \\(\\bar z\\) is nonzero. Write \\(\\eta\\) for \\(K(n)\\)-localization and \\(\\lambda\\) for \\(L_{n-1}\\)-localization. Naturality gives\n\\[\n\\eta_Xc\\simeq L_{K(n)}(c)\\eta_S,\n\\qquad\ni_{n,X}\\lambda_X\\simeq\\lambda_{L_{K(n)}X}\\eta_X.\n\\]\nThese are the two naturality squares used in original Proposition 2.2. The first sends \\(z\\) to zero; the second then gives\n\\[\n\\pi_q(i_{n,X})(\\bar z)=0.\n\\]\nA retraction would induce a left inverse on this homotopy group and is therefore impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(c\\) is a \\(K(a)\\)-equivalence.", "statement": "<p>**Completion comparison — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c:S\\to X=S_p^\\wedge\\) be the derived \\(p\\)-completion map.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\ge1\\), and let \\(1\\le t\\le r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(c\\) is a \\(K(a)\\)-equivalence. Every map from \\(S\\) to a \\(K(t)\\)-local spectrum \\(Y\\) factors through\n\\[\nS\\longrightarrow X\\longrightarrow L_rX.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the inverse limit of the cofiber sequences\n\\[\nS\\xrightarrow{p^j}S\\longrightarrow S/p^j,\n\\]\nwith multiplication by \\(p\\) on the left spheres and identity on the middle spheres. The fiber of \\(c\\) is\n\\[\nC\\simeq\\operatorname*{holim}(\\cdots\\xrightarrow pS\\xrightarrow pS)\n\\simeq F(S[1/p],S).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(p\\) is an equivalence on this function spectrum, because it is an equivalence on its first argument. It remains an equivalence after ordinary smash with \\(K(a)\\). But\n\\[\n\\pi_*(K(a)\\wedge C)\n\\]\nis a module over \\(K(a)_*\\) and is annihilated by \\(p\\). Thus it is zero, proving the first claim.</p><p>A map to a \\(K(t)\\)-local target consequently extends across \\(c\\). The target is also \\(L_r\\)-local when \\(t\\le r\\), since an \\(L_r\\)-acyclic spectrum is \\(K(t)\\)-acyclic. The extension therefore factors through \\(L_rX\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "This spectral sequence has the cup product on \\(E_2\\), and its differentials satisfy the derivation rule.", "statement": "<p>**Ordinary filtered products — original Lemma 3.2, proved in Appendix A.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a finite group \\(Q\\) act coherently on a commutative ring spectrum \\(R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Form the ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence\n  \\[\n  E_2^{s,t}=H^s(Q;\\pi_tR).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This spectral sequence has the cup product on \\(E_2\\), and its differentials satisfy the derivation rule.</p><p>Whenever it converges to \\(\\pi_*R^{hQ}\\) with a separated homotopy filtration whose successive quotients are \\(E_\\infty\\), its product on \\(E_\\infty\\) is the associated-graded product for that actual homotopy filtration.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the homogeneous free bar construction \\(EQ_\\bullet\\), with \\(EQ_q=Q^{q+1}\\), and let \\(EQ^{(s)}\\) be its \\(s\\)-skeleton. The full totalization tower of the group-action resolution is\n\\[\nV_s=F_Q((EQ^{(s)})_+,R),\\qquad\n\\operatorname*{holim}_sV_s=F_Q(EQ_+,R)=R^{hQ},\n\\]\nwhere \\(F_Q\\) denotes the derived spectrum of equivariant maps. The skeletal filtration gives homogeneous cochains and hence the stated \\(E_2\\)-page.</p><p>For barycentric coordinates \\(t=(t_0,\\ldots,t_q)\\in\\Delta^q\\), set\n\\[\nc_{-1}=0,\\qquad c_i=t_0+\\cdots+t_i,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\n\\ell_i(t)=2\\bigl(\\min(c_i,\\tfrac12)-\\min(c_{i-1},\\tfrac12)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho_i(t)=2\\bigl(\\max(c_i,\\tfrac12)-\\max(c_{i-1},\\tfrac12)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThese vectors are nonnegative and each has sum one. Their coordinates are twice the lengths of the intersections of \\([c_{i-1},c_i]\\) with \\([0,\\frac12]\\) and \\([\\frac12,1]\\), respectively. The formulas are continuous, including when a cumulative sum equals \\(\\frac12\\).</p><p>For an order-preserving map \\(\\theta:[q]\\to[q']\\), write\n\\[\n(\\theta_*t)_j=\\sum_{\\theta(i)=j}t_i.\n\\]\nEach interval belonging to \\(\\theta_*t\\) is the union of the consecutive intervals belonging to the corresponding fiber of \\(\\theta\\); an empty fiber gives an interval of length zero. Additivity of intersection lengths gives\n\\[\n\\ell(\\theta_*t)=\\theta_*\\ell(t),\\qquad\n\\rho(\\theta_*t)=\\theta_*\\rho(t).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\delta[\\sigma,t]=([\\sigma,\\ell(t)],[\\sigma,\\rho(t)])\n\\]\nis compatible with all faces and degeneracies and defines a continuous \\(Q\\)-equivariant map\n\\[\n\\delta:EQ\\longrightarrow EQ\\times EQ.\n\\]\nThe formula\n\\[\n[\\sigma,t]\\longmapsto\n\\bigl([\\sigma,(1-\\tau)t+\\tau\\ell(t)],\n      [\\sigma,(1-\\tau)t+\\tau\\rho(t)]\\bigr),\n\\qquad 0\\le\\tau\\le1,\n\\]\ndefines an equivariant homotopy from the ordinary diagonal to \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>Choose \\(k\\) with \\(c_{k-1}\\le\\frac12\\le c_k\\). The support of \\(\\ell(t)\\) is contained in the front face \\([0,\\ldots,k]\\), and the support of \\(\\rho(t)\\) is contained in the back face \\([k,\\ldots,q]\\). Their dimensions are \\(k\\) and \\(q-k\\); degenerate faces can only lower these dimensions. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\delta(EQ^{(s)})\\subset\n\\bigcup_{i+j\\le s}EQ^{(i)}\\times EQ^{(j)}.\n\\]</p><p>On the region \\(c_{k-1}\\le\\frac12\\le c_k\\), the map\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto(\\ell(t),\\rho(t))\n\\]\nis a homeomorphism onto \\(\\Delta^k\\times\\Delta^{q-k}\\), with inverse\n\\[\nt_i=\\ell_i/2\\quad(i&lt;k),\\qquad\nt_k=(\\ell_k+\\rho_k)/2,\\qquad\nt_i=\\rho_i/2\\quad(i&gt;k).\n\\]\nIn oriented coordinates \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_q\\) on the source and\n\\[\n\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_k,\\rho_{k+1},\\ldots,\\rho_q\n\\]\non the product, its Jacobian determinant is \\(2^q&gt;0\\). The regions meet on their boundaries, so the cellular fundamental chain maps to\n\\[\n[\\sigma]\\longmapsto\n\\sum_{k=0}^q\n[\\sigma|_{[0,\\ldots,k]}]\\otimes\n[\\sigma|_{[k,\\ldots,q]}],\n\\]\nwith degenerate terms omitted in normalized chains. This is the Alexander–Whitney diagonal.</p><p>Write \\(EQ/EQ^{(s)}\\) for the based cofiber of\n\\[\n(EQ^{(s)})_+\\longrightarrow EQ_+,\n\\]\nand set \\(EQ^{(-1)}=\\varnothing\\). The skeletal inclusions are \\(Q\\)-CW cofibrations, so these quotients represent homotopy cofibers. For \\(a,b\\ge0\\), the skeletal containment makes the composite of \\(\\delta\\) with the quotient to\n\\[\n(EQ/EQ^{(a-1)})\\wedge(EQ/EQ^{(b-1)})\n\\]\nconstant on \\(EQ^{(a+b-1)}\\). Hence there is a relative map\n\\[\nEQ/EQ^{(a+b-1)}\n\\longrightarrow\n(EQ/EQ^{(a-1)})\\wedge(EQ/EQ^{(b-1)}).\n\\]\nMultiplication on \\(R\\) and precomposition give compatible products\n\\[\nF_Q(EQ/EQ^{(a-1)},R)\\wedge\nF_Q(EQ/EQ^{(b-1)},R)\n\\longrightarrow\nF_Q(EQ/EQ^{(a+b-1)},R).\n\\]</p><p>The associativity needed for powers also holds within this relative filtration. Partition \\([0,1]\\) into \\(m\\) successive intervals of positive lengths. For the \\(j\\)-th output vector, divide the lengths of its intersections with \\([c_{i-1},c_i]\\) by the length of the \\(j\\)-th interval. Naturality and equivariance hold as above. The supports lie in successive faces\n\\[\n[0,\\ldots,k_1],\\ [k_1,\\ldots,k_2],\\ \\ldots,\\\n[k_{m-1},\\ldots,q],\n\\]\nwhose dimensions sum to \\(q\\); boundary cases can only reduce this sum. Composing cut maps is refinement of the partition. The positive interval lengths form a convex simplex, so partitions for different parenthesizations are joined through maps with the same relative skeletal property. Thus the products are associative up to filtered homotopy.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nI_s=F_Q(EQ/EQ^{(s-1)},R),\\qquad s\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe quotient cofiber sequences give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cofib}(I_{s+1}\\to I_s)\n\\simeq F_Q(EQ^{(s)}/EQ^{(s-1)},R)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{fib}(V_s\\to V_{s-1}).\n\\]\nThe fiber sequences\n\\[\nI_s\\longrightarrow R^{hQ}\\longrightarrow V_{s-1}\n\\]\nidentify the connecting maps with those of the \\(V_s\\)-tower, with the usual rotation signs. The paired relative spectral sequence is therefore the ordinary tower spectral sequence with the same filtration index.</p><p>On cellular cochains, the displayed chain formula is the ordinary cup product. In inhomogeneous coordinates,\n\\[\n(v\\smile w)(g_1,\\ldots,g_{a+b})\n=\nv(g_1,\\ldots,g_a)\\,\n(g_1\\cdots g_a)w(g_{a+1},\\ldots,g_{a+b}),\n\\]\nwith the usual graded signs. The cellular boundary formula for a product, together with the internal Koszul sign, gives the derivation rule in the paired exact couples and hence on every page. Subsequent products are induced on homology, so they agree on all later pages.</p><p>Finally, the image of \\(\\pi_*I_a\\) in \\(\\pi_*R^{hQ}\\) is\n\\[\n\\ker\\bigl(\\pi_*R^{hQ}\\to\\pi_*V_{a-1}\\bigr).\n\\]\nAfter forgetting relative conditions, the constructed product is homotopic to the actual product by the homotopy from \\(\\delta\\) to the diagonal. It therefore multiplies the actual filtration groups. Passing to successive relative quotients gives the spectral-sequence product. Under the stated convergence hypothesis, those quotients are \\(E_\\infty\\), proving the associated-graded assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The image of \\(z_p\\) is nonzero in each of \\( \\pi_{dh^2}L_{K(h)}S,\\qquad \\pi_{dh^2}L_{K(h)}X,\\qquad \\pi_{dh^2}L_rX\\quad(r\\ge h). \\) All three images are induced by the sphere unit and, for the completed input, by the …", "statement": "<p>**The lower sphere product — original Theorem 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be an odd prime and put\n  \\[\n  h=p-1,\\qquad d=2p(p-1)-2,\\qquad k=h^2,\\qquad\n  z_p=\\beta_1^{h^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_h\\) be Morava \\(E\\)-theory over \\(\\mathbb F_{p^h}\\), and let \\(X=S_p^\\wedge\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image of \\(z_p\\) is nonzero in each of\n\\[\n\\pi_{dh^2}L_{K(h)}S,\\qquad\n\\pi_{dh^2}L_{K(h)}X,\\qquad\n\\pi_{dh^2}L_rX\\quad(r\\ge h).\n\\]\nAll three images are induced by the sphere unit and, for the completed input, by the completion map \\(S\\to X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The spectrum \\(Y\\) is \\(K(h)\\)-local, since \\(E_h\\) is local and local objects are closed under homotopy limits. Its unit factors through \\(L_{K(h)}S\\), where the image of \\(z_p\\) must be nonzero by Lemma 3.2.</p><p>Proposition 1 makes\n\\[\nL_{K(h)}(S\\to X)\n\\]\nan equivalence; naturality carries the nonzero image to \\(L_{K(h)}X\\).</p><p>For every \\(r\\ge h\\), Proposition 1 factors the detecting unit through\n\\[\nS\\longrightarrow X\\longrightarrow L_rX\\longrightarrow Y.\n\\]\nIts nonzero value on \\(z_p\\) proves the remaining assertion.</p><p>At \\(p=5\\), this is the named class \\(\\beta_1^{16}\\) in degree\n\\[\n38\\cdot16=608.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence is strongly convergent and has a separated filtration \\( F^s\\pi_qY/F^{s+1}\\pi_qY\\cong E_\\infty^{s,q+s}. \\) Its positive-filtration \\(E_2\\)-page is the corresponding …", "statement": "<p>**The actual unit and the Hopkins–Miller calculation used in original Section 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(p,h,d\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  G_h=S_h\\rtimes\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{p^h}/\\mathbb F_p)\n  \\]\n  for the extended stabilizer.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the finite subgroup \\(G\\subset G_h\\) whose intersection with \\(S_h\\) is\n  \\[\n  C_p\\rtimes C_{h^2}\n  \\]\n  and whose Galois projection is onto, as in Heard [10, Section 2].</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  Y=E_h^{hG},\\qquad \\eta:S\\to Y,\n  \\]\n  where \\(Y\\) is the ordinary homotopy fixed point spectrum and \\(\\eta\\) is its commutative-ring unit.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence is strongly convergent and has a separated filtration\n\\[\nF^s\\pi_qY/F^{s+1}\\pi_qY\\cong E_\\infty^{s,q+s}.\n\\]\nIts positive-filtration \\(E_2\\)-page is the corresponding part of\n\\[\n\\mathbb F_p[\\alpha,\\beta,\\Delta^{\\pm1}]/(\\alpha^2),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n|\\alpha|=(1,2h),\\qquad\n|\\beta|=(2,2ph),\\qquad\n|\\Delta|=(0,2ph^2).\n\\]\nFor some \\(c\\in\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\), the actual unit satisfies\n\\[\n\\eta_*(\\beta_1)\\in F^2\\pi_dY,\\qquad\n[\\eta_*(\\beta_1)]=c\\beta\\in E_\\infty^{2,2ph}.\n\\]\nIn positive even filtration,\n\\[\nE_\\infty^{\\mathrm{even},*}\n\\cong\n\\mathbb F_p[\\Delta^{\\pm p}][\\beta]/(\\beta^{h^2+1}),\n\\]\nso \\(\\beta^{h^2}\\ne0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Devinatz–Hopkins identify the ordinary finite-group fixed-point spectral sequence with the local Adams spectral sequence and give strong convergence [7, Theorems 2(ii) and 3].</p><p>The positive-filtration \\(E_2\\)-calculation is the Hopkins–Miller calculation recorded in Heard [10, Proposition 5.5] and Heard–Mathew–Stojanoska [11, Proposition 2.7].</p><p>Heard identifies \\(\\beta\\) with the image of the named stable element \\(\\beta_1\\) under the actual unit [10, proof of Theorem 5.6]. Allowing for the choice of cohomology generator gives the scalar \\(c\\). The Ext-to-group-cohomology comparison and permanence are also recorded after [11, Proposition 2.7].</p><p>The positive even-filtration \\(E_\\infty\\)-calculation is Heard [10, Lemma 5.8]. These inputs hold for every odd prime and concern the extended group \\(G\\) and \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The actual unit carries \\( z_p=\\beta_1^{h^2} \\) to a nonzero class in \\(\\pi_{dh^2}Y\\).", "statement": "<p>**The actual product in the detector — original Proposition 3.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the detector\n  \\[\n  \\eta:S\\to Y=E_h^{hG}\n  \\]\n  and its filtration from Lemma 3.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The actual unit carries\n\\[\nz_p=\\beta_1^{h^2}\n\\]\nto a nonzero class in \\(\\pi_{dh^2}Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 2 to the ordinary spectral sequence in Lemma 3.1. The class of\n\\[\n\\eta_*(\\beta_1)^{h^2}\n\\]\nin\n\\[\nF^{2h^2}\\pi_{dh^2}Y/F^{2h^2+1}\\pi_{dh^2}Y\n\\]\nis\n\\[\nc^{h^2}\\beta^{h^2}\\ne0.\n\\]\nHence the homotopy class itself is nonzero. Since \\(\\eta\\) is a ring map, this power is exactly\n\\[\n\\eta_*(\\beta_1^{h^2}),\n\\]\nthe image of the specified sphere product.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The ordinary smash product has even homotopy \\( \\pi_*(E_a\\wedge E_b) \\cong (E_a)_*\\otimes_{BP_*}BP_*BP\\otimes_{BP_*}(E_b)_*. \\) This module is flat over \\((E_a)_*\\) through the first factor.", "statement": "<p>**Ordinary mixed cooperations — original Lemma 8.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a,b\\) be positive heights.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_a,E_b\\) be the corresponding Morava \\(E\\)-theories.</p><p class=\"li\">All smash products in this statement are ordinary.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(BP\\) be the \\(p\\)-local Brown–Peterson spectrum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ordinary smash product has even homotopy\n\\[\n\\pi_*(E_a\\wedge E_b)\n\\cong\n(E_a)_*\\otimes_{BP_*}BP_*BP\\otimes_{BP_*}(E_b)_*.\n\\]\nThis module is flat over \\((E_a)_*\\) through the first factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the ordinary Landweber cooperation calculation to the \\(p\\)-typical flat Hopf algebroid\n\\[\n(BP_*,BP_*BP).\n\\]\nThis gives the displayed formula. It is the \\(p\\)-typical form of the \\(MU_*\\)-formulas in Rezk [26, Section 15, Proposition 15.1 and Corollary 15.4] and Hovey [14, Lemma 4.4]; Morava \\(E\\)-theory over \\(BP\\) is included in Hovey–Strickland [16, Example 0.1(d)].</p><p>The two tensor products use the two different unit maps into \\(BP_*BP\\). For a Landweber-exact \\(BP_*\\)-algebra \\(C\\), the module\n\\[\nBP_*BP\\otimes_{BP_*}C\n\\]\nis flat over the other \\(BP_*\\)-action. Indeed, tensoring with it factors as the exact cofree-comodule functor followed by the exact Landweber functor on comodules. This is also the opposite-unit criterion of [16, Lemma 2.2], with conjugation exchanging the units when necessary.</p><p>Base change along \\(BP_*\\to(E_a)_*\\) proves flatness. The coefficient and cooperation rings are even graded, proving evenness. This argument does not require \\((E_b)_*\\) to be flat over \\(BP_*\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The actual localization map induces \\( U_*\\longrightarrow\\pi_*L_{K(a)}N \\cong \\widehat U_*:=\\varprojlim_jU_*/\\mathfrak m^jU_*. \\) For every flat \\(\\mathcal O\\)-module \\(U\\) and every \\(j\\ge1\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Flat completion and reduction — original Lemma 8.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive height \\(a\\), put \\(E=E_a\\), and write\n  \\[\n  E_*=\\mathcal O[u^{\\pm1}],\\qquad |u|=-2,\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal O=W(\\mathbb F_{p^a})[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}]],\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathfrak m=(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N\\) be an ordinary \\(E\\)-module with flat graded homotopy \\(U_*=\\pi_*N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The actual localization map induces\n\\[\nU_*\\longrightarrow\\pi_*L_{K(a)}N\n\\cong\n\\widehat U_*:=\\varprojlim_jU_*/\\mathfrak m^jU_*.\n\\]\nFor every flat \\(\\mathcal O\\)-module \\(U\\) and every \\(j\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat U/\\mathfrak m^j\\widehat U\\cong U/\\mathfrak m^jU.\n\\]\nBoth assertions apply degreewise to the periodic graded modules above.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first assertion is the localization theorem for ordinary Morava \\(E\\)-modules: \\(K(a)\\)-localization is derived \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-completion, and flat homotopy has no higher derived completion. The natural localization map induces the ordinary completion map [27, Propositions 3.4, 3.6 and Corollary 3.8].</p><p>For the reduction assertion, let \\(N_0\\) be a finitely generated \\(\\mathcal O\\)-module and choose a resolution\n\\[\nP_\\bullet\\longrightarrow N_0\n\\]\nby finite free modules. The tower\n\\[\nP_\\bullet\\otimes_{\\mathcal O}U/\\mathfrak m^lU\n\\]\nis degreewise surjective. Flatness of \\(U\\) identifies its first homology with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}^{\\mathcal O}_1(N_0,\\mathcal O/\\mathfrak m^l)\n\\otimes_{\\mathcal O}U.\n\\]\nThis homology tower is pro-zero. For a finite first presentation\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K\\longrightarrow P_0\\longrightarrow N_0\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nthe corresponding Tor group is\n\\[\n(K\\cap\\mathfrak m^lP_0)/\\mathfrak m^lK.\n\\]\nArtin–Rees [30, Tag 00IN] gives \\(d\\) such that\n\\[\nK\\cap\\mathfrak m^lP_0\\subseteq\\mathfrak m^{l-d}K\n\\quad(l\\ge d).\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(j\\), a transition from \\(l\\ge j+d\\) to \\(j\\) is therefore zero. Tensoring these zero transitions with \\(U\\) preserves that conclusion.</p><p>The homology exact sequence for the countable inverse limit of the degreewise-surjective tower gives\n\\[\nN_0\\otimes_{\\mathcal O}\\widehat U\n\\cong\n\\varprojlim_l\n\\bigl(N_0\\otimes_{\\mathcal O}U/\\mathfrak m^lU\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe possible \\(\\varprojlim^1\\) of first homology is zero because the tower is pro-zero. Finite freeness of the \\(P_i\\) identifies tensoring them with \\(\\widehat U\\) with their inverse-limit tensor.</p><p>Taking \\(N_0=\\mathcal O/\\mathfrak m^j\\), the right-hand modules are \\(U/\\mathfrak m^jU\\) for \\(l\\ge j\\). This proves\n\\[\n\\widehat U/\\mathfrak m^j\\widehat U\\cong U/\\mathfrak m^jU.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(W\\), \\( \\pi_*(Z_r\\wedge W) \\cong C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a^r,\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)). \\) The first \\(E\\)-factor acts by constant coefficients.", "statement": "<p>**The finite-test coefficient complex — original Proposition 8.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive height \\(a\\), and write\n  \\[\n  E=E_a,\\qquad k_a=\\mathbb F_{p^a},\\qquad\n  \\mathcal O=W(k_a)[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}]],\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E_*=\\mathcal O[u^{\\pm1}],\\qquad |u|=-2,\\qquad\n  \\mathfrak m=(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the coherent action of\n  \\[\n  G_a=S_a\\rtimes\\operatorname{Gal}(k_a/\\mathbb F_p)\n  \\]\n  by commutative ring automorphisms, with the prescribed Honda stabilizer action on the special fiber.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_a=\\mathrm{Sp}_{K(a)},\\qquad\n  B_a=L_{K(a)}S,\\qquad\n  U\\otimes_aV=L_{K(a)}(U\\wedge V),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  Z_r=L_{K(a)}(E^{\\wedge(r+1)}),\\qquad r\\ge0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Functions and coefficient topologies below are understood degreewise, with the full \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-adic topology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(W\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_*(Z_r\\wedge W)\n\\cong\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a^r,\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)).\n\\]\nThe first \\(E\\)-factor acts by constant coefficients. The two-factor evaluation at \\(g\\) is multiplication after \\(1\\wedge g\\).</p><p>In cumulative coordinates\n\\[\n1,g_1,g_1g_2,\\ldots,g_1\\cdots g_r,\n\\]\nthese isomorphisms identify every coface and codegeneracy of the unit Amitsur object with the continuous inhomogeneous group-cochain operator.</p><p>Consequently, for a finite ordinary spectrum \\(V_0\\), applying the exact ordinary functor \\(F(V_0,-)\\) to the full local unit Amitsur tower gives\n\\[\nE_2^{s,q}\n=\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s\\bigl(G_a;\\pi_q(E_a\\wedge DV_0)\\bigr),\n\\qquad DV_0=F(V_0,S).\n\\]\nThe resulting Morava action on these coefficients is continuous, and their quotients by powers of \\(\\mathfrak m\\) are continuous discrete \\(G_a\\)-modules.</p>", "proof": "<p>The completed cooperation theorem gives\n\\[\n\\pi_*L_{K(a)}(E\\wedge E)\n\\cong C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a,E_*).\n\\]\nAt \\(g\\), the map is \\(\\mu(1\\wedge g)\\), and the first factor acts by constant scalars. This is the evaluation convention of Hovey [14, Section 1 and Theorems 4.11 and 6.8]. The equivalent convention in Devinatz–Hopkins [7, Proposition 2.2 and Equations (2.3), (2.5)–(2.7)] uses \\(\\mu(g^{-1}\\wedge1)\\); exchange of factors and inversion of the group coordinate give the convention above.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nP_*=\\pi_*(E\\wedge E),\n\\]\nas an \\(E_*\\)-module through the first factor. Proposition 4 makes \\(P_*\\) flat. Proposition 5 and Lemma 6.1, applied to \\(E_*/\\mathfrak m^jE_*\\), give\n\\[\nP_*/\\mathfrak m^jP_*\n\\cong\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a,E_*/\\mathfrak m^jE_*).\n\\]\nThis isomorphism is induced by the actual ordinary evaluation\n\\[\nE\\wedge E\\xrightarrow{1\\wedge g}E\\wedge E\\xrightarrow\\mu E\n\\]\nfollowed by reduction.</p><p>For higher powers, use the common first factor in\n\\[\n(E_0\\wedge E_1)\\wedge_{E_0}\\cdots\n\\wedge_{E_0}(E_0\\wedge E_r)\n\\simeq E_0\\wedge E_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge E_r.\n\\]\nIterating Lemma 6.1, including its extension to arbitrary ordinary spectra, identifies the homotopy of the ordinary smash with\n\\[\nP_*^{\\otimes_{E_*}r}.\n\\]\nThe comparison inserts each pair into the first and one other position and multiplies at the common position. These modules are flat.</p><p>Degreewise, \\(E_*/\\mathfrak m^jE_*\\) is finite. Continuous functions into it are unions of function modules on finite quotient sets. Tensor products of function modules on finite sets are function modules on their products, and every continuous function on \\(G_a^r\\) into a finite set factors through finite quotients in the coordinates. Hence\n\\[\nP_*^{\\otimes_{E_*}r}/\\mathfrak m^j\n\\cong\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a^r,E_*/\\mathfrak m^jE_*).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 5 to the ordinary \\((r+1)\\)-fold smash only at this point. It gives\n\\[\n\\pi_*Z_r\\cong C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a^r,E_*).\n\\]\nFor \\(r=0\\), this is the locality of \\(E\\).</p><p>These maps remain the evaluations on the non-first factors followed by multiplication. Each ordinary evaluation extends through localization because its target \\(E\\) is local. On homotopy, first-factor linearity makes it adically continuous. Proposition 5 makes the ordinary image dense, and the reductions show that the extension is the displayed continuous function.</p><p>For finite \\(W\\), the ordinary \\(E\\)-module \\(Z_r\\) satisfies the exactness assumption of Lemma 6.1, by the continuous-function formula. Applying that lemma gives\n\\[\n\\pi_*(Z_r\\wedge W)\n\\cong C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G_a^r,\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)).\n\\]\nThis allows torsion and both parities in \\(\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)\\).</p><p>The local unit Amitsur terms are \\(Z_r\\). Ordinary duality gives\n\\[\nF(V_0,Z_r)\\simeq Z_r\\wedge DV_0\n\\]\nfor finite \\(V_0\\). Relabel the independent evaluations by cumulative coordinates\n\\[\n1,g_1,g_1g_2,\\ldots,g_1\\cdots g_r.\n\\]\nThe cochain identities can be checked on ordinary evaluations before localization. Their targets \\(E\\wedge W\\) are \\(K(a)\\)-local for finite \\(W\\), since finite dualizable smash preserves local objects, so the identities extend through localization.</p><p>Explicitly, for a degree-\\(r\\) cochain \\(c\\),\n\\[\n(\\delta^0c)(g_1,\\ldots,g_{r+1})\n=g_1c(g_2,\\ldots,g_{r+1}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\delta^ic)(g_1,\\ldots,g_{r+1})\n=c(g_1,\\ldots,g_ig_{i+1},\\ldots,g_{r+1})\n\\quad(1\\le i\\le r),\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\delta^{r+1}c)(g_1,\\ldots,g_{r+1})\n=c(g_1,\\ldots,g_r).\n\\]\nMultiplication of adjacent factors gives the codegeneracies, inserting the identity in the corresponding coordinate.</p><p>The first unit insertion displays each coefficient’s orbit function. These functions are continuous, and reduction gives continuous actions on the finite invariant quotients by powers of \\(\\mathfrak m\\). Cosimplicial cohomology of the homotopy groups is therefore the asserted continuous group cohomology \\(E_2\\)-page.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(W\\), the natural comparison \\( \\pi_*D_0\\otimes_{E_*}\\pi_*(E\\wedge W) \\longrightarrow \\pi_*(D_0\\wedge W) \\) is an isomorphism.", "statement": "<p>**Base change and continuous functions — original Lemma 8.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(E,\\mathcal O,\\mathfrak m\\) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_0\\) be an ordinary right \\(E\\)-module such that\n  \\[\n  \\pi_*D_0\\otimes_{E_*}(-)\n  \\]\n  is exact on finitely generated graded \\(E_*\\)-modules.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a profinite set.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(W\\), the natural comparison\n\\[\n\\pi_*D_0\\otimes_{E_*}\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\pi_*(D_0\\wedge W)\n\\]\nis an isomorphism.</p><p>The functor \\(C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T,-)\\) is exact on finitely generated \\(\\mathcal O\\)-modules with their \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-adic topologies. For every finitely generated graded \\(E_*\\)-module \\(M_*\\),\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T,E_*)\\otimes_{E_*}M_*\n\\xrightarrow{\\ \\cong\\ }\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T,M_*).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First suppose \\(W\\) is finite. The module\n\\[\nM_*=\\pi_*(E\\wedge W)\n\\]\nis finitely generated over \\(E_*\\), by finite cofiber constructions and retracts from the sphere case. Periodicity reduces graded module questions to the two parities over \\(\\mathcal O\\). Since \\(\\mathcal O\\) is complete, Noetherian, regular, and local, regularity supplies a finite resolution by finite graded free \\(E_*\\)-modules [30, Tag 00O7].</p><p>There is an equality of ordinary derived constructions\n\\[\nD_0\\wedge W\\simeq D_0\\wedge_E(E\\wedge W).\n\\]\nThe comparison is the balanced homotopy cross product, natural before any resolution is chosen. Realize a finite graded free surjection onto \\(M_*\\) by an \\(E\\)-module map from a finite wedge of suspensions of \\(E\\). Surjectivity makes the homotopy of its fiber the first syzygy. Repeat along a finite free resolution. At the last stage, lifts of a free basis give an equivalence from a finite free \\(E\\)-module, since they induce an isomorphism on all homotopy groups.</p><p>Relative smash with \\(D_0\\) is exact. Induction back through these fiber sequences, using tensor exactness on each finitely generated syzygy, proves the comparison for finite \\(W\\).</p><p>Every ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum is a filtered homotopy colimit of finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectra. Both sides preserve those colimits, so naturality extends the comparison to arbitrary \\(W\\).</p><p>For continuous functions, let \\(M\\to N\\) be a surjection of finitely generated \\(\\mathcal O\\)-modules. For every \\(j\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nM/\\mathfrak m^{j+1}M\n\\longrightarrow\n(M/\\mathfrak m^jM)\n\\times_{N/\\mathfrak m^jN}\n(N/\\mathfrak m^{j+1}N)\n\\]\nis surjective. Lift the first component to \\(M\\); its error against the second lies in \\(\\mathfrak m^jN\\), and that error lifts from \\(\\mathfrak m^jM\\).</p><p>Each quotient is finite. Given a continuous map \\(T\\to N\\), lift first modulo \\(\\mathfrak m\\), then successively through these surjections while preserving the preceding choices. A lift on each finite value set is continuous. The compatible system defines a continuous lift to \\(M\\), since finitely generated modules are complete.</p><p>Kernels carry their subspace topologies: the modules are compact Hausdorff, and the induced continuous bijection from a kernel to its image is a homeomorphism. Thus \\(C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T,-)\\) is exact.</p><p>Apply this exact functor to a finite presentation of \\(M\\), and compare with tensoring the presentation by \\(C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T,\\mathcal O)\\). The comparison is an isomorphism on finite free terms and hence on \\(M\\). Apply the argument in each parity to obtain the graded assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(q\\), \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_a;M)=0 \\qquad(s>a^2+1). \\)", "statement": "<p>**The two upper cohomological bounds — original Proposition 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let either \\(a=p\\) with \\(p\\ge5\\), or \\(a=p+1\\) with \\(p\\ge7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  (E_a)_*=\\mathcal O_a[u^{\\pm1}],\\qquad\n  \\mathcal O_a=W(k_a)[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}]],\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  k_a=\\mathbb F_{p^a},\\qquad\n  \\mathfrak m_a=(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  G_a=S_a\\rtimes\\operatorname{Gal}(k_a/\\mathbb F_p).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_0\\) be a finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum, put \\(DV_0=F(V_0,S)\\), and give\n  \\[\n  M=\\pi_q(E_a\\wedge DV_0)\n  \\]\n  its \\(\\mathfrak m_a\\)-adic topology and Morava action.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(q\\),\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_a;M)=0\n\\qquad(s&gt;a^2+1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The pure stabilizer \\(S_a\\) is the maximal-order unit group in the central division algebra over \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\) of dimension \\(a^2\\), so it is compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic of that dimension [24, Sections 2.1–2.2].</p><p>It has no element \\(g\\) of order \\(p\\) in either stated range. Indeed,\n\\[\n(g-1)\\Phi_p(g)=0\n\\]\nin a division algebra, and \\(g\\ne1\\) implies \\(\\Phi_p(g)=0\\). Since \\(\\Phi_p(X+1)\\) is Eisenstein, \\(g\\) generates the degree-\\((p-1)\\) subfield \\(\\mathbb Q_p(\\zeta_p)\\). The division algebra would be a vector space over this subfield, forcing\n\\[\np-1\\mid a^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(a=p\\), this forces \\(p-1\\mid1\\); for \\(a=p+1\\), it forces \\(p-1\\mid4\\). Both are impossible in the stated ranges. The Lazard–Serre theorem therefore gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cd}_p(S_a)=a^2\n\\]\nfor discrete \\(p\\)-primary coefficients [29, Section 1, Corollary (1)].</p><p>For each \\(q\\), \\(M\\) is finitely generated over the complete Noetherian ring \\(\\mathcal O_a\\), by the finite construction of \\(DV_0\\). Hence it is complete and separated, with finite discrete \\(p\\)-primary quotients\n\\[\nM_j=M/\\mathfrak m_a^jM\n\\]\nand surjective transitions. This requires no flatness and allows torsion and both parities. The maximal ideal is invariant. Continuity for sphere coefficients is part of the Morava action in Devinatz–Hopkins [7, Theorem 1(ii) and Remark 1.3]; for every finite \\(V_0\\), the actual action and continuity are supplied by Proposition 6.</p><p>The pure stabilizer fixes \\(W(k_a)\\). It fixes the residue field, hence the unique Hensel lifts of the roots of \\(X^{p^a}-X\\), the Teichmüller representatives; continuity then fixes their Witt expansions. Thus \\(S_a\\) acts Witt-linearly and the Galois group acts semilinearly.</p><p>At \\(a=p\\), apply Lemma 7.2 with \\(D=p^2\\). Although the full group has a split Galois subgroup of order \\(p\\), Lemma 7.1 applies to these coefficients. The cohomological-dimension theorem has been applied only to \\(S_p\\).</p><p>At \\(a=p+1\\), the Galois quotient has order \\(p+1\\), prime to \\(p\\). An order-\\(p\\) element of \\(G_a\\) would lie in \\(S_a\\), where it has been excluded. The full group is compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic of dimension \\(a^2\\), so its discrete \\(p\\)-primary cohomology vanishes above \\(a^2\\). Apply the inverse-limit part of Lemma 7.2.</p><p>Both cases give the claimed bound above \\(a^2+1\\), retaining the possible adic inverse-limit contribution in degree \\(a^2+1\\).</p><p>For comparison, original Remark 4.4 cites the sharper sphere-coefficient line of Bobkova and collaborators [5, Equation (2) and Remark 1.2] when \\(p-1\\nmid a\\); at \\(p=a=5\\), it gives vanishing for \\(s&gt;25\\). That statement is not used as a bound for all finite-test modules.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H^i(Q;P)=0\\qquad(i>0), \\) without any finiteness or flatness hypothesis on \\(P\\).", "statement": "<p>**Trace-one contraction — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In place of the height-specific setting of Proposition 7, let a finite group \\(Q\\) act on a commutative ring \\(W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(w\\in W\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{g\\in Q}g(w)=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be any semilinear \\(W\\)-module.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH^i(Q;P)=0\\qquad(i&gt;0),\n\\]\nwithout any finiteness or flatness hypothesis on \\(P\\).</p><p>In particular, the conclusion holds for\n\\[\nW=W(\\mathbb F_{p^a}),\\qquad\nQ=\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{p^a}/\\mathbb F_p),\n\\]\nwithout a prime-to-\\(p\\) hypothesis on \\(a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use homogeneous group cochains: a degree-\\(q\\) cochain is an equivariant function\n\\[\nf:Q^{q+1}\\to P\n\\]\nwith differential the alternating deletion of coordinates. For \\(q\\ge1\\), define\n\\[\n(hf)(g_0,\\ldots,g_{q-1})\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in Q}\\gamma(w)\nf(\\gamma,g_0,\\ldots,g_{q-1}).\n\\]\nReplacing \\(\\gamma\\) by \\(g\\gamma\\) proves equivariance: semilinearity converts\n\\[\n(g\\gamma)(w)\\,gf\n\\]\ninto \\(g(\\gamma(w)f)\\). In \\(hd+dh\\), the terms deleting one of the displayed \\(g_i\\) cancel in pairs. The term deleting the inserted coordinate is\n\\[\n\\sum_\\gamma\\gamma(w)f=f.\n\\]\nThus \\(hd+dh=\\mathrm{id}\\) in positive degrees.</p><p>For Witt vectors, the finite-field trace is onto. Lift a residue trace-one element to \\(w_0\\in W\\). Its Witt trace lies in \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\) and is congruent to one modulo \\(p\\), hence is a unit. Divide \\(w_0\\) by this invariant unit to obtain \\(w\\). No division by \\(|Q|\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M_j)=0\\quad(s>D), \\) and \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M)=0\\quad(s>D+1). \\) The second conclusion also holds if the finite-quotient vanishing is supplied directly, without the semidirect-product …", "statement": "<p>**Finite quotients and the extra adic degree — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In place of the height-specific setting, let\n  \\[\n  G=S_0\\rtimes Q\n  \\]\n  be profinite, with \\(Q\\) finite.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\ge0\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{cd}_p(S_0)\\le D\n  \\]\n  for discrete \\(p\\)-primary modules.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\{M_j\\}_j\\) be a countable tower of finite discrete \\(p\\)-primary \\(G\\)-modules with surjective transitions, and set\n  \\[\n  M=\\varprojlim_jM_j\n  \\]\n  with its inverse-limit topology.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose each \\(M_j\\) is a module over a commutative ring \\(W\\), the group \\(S_0\\) acts \\(W\\)-linearly, \\(Q\\) acts semilinearly, and \\(W\\) has a trace-one element as in Lemma 7.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M_j)=0\\quad(s&gt;D),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M)=0\\quad(s&gt;D+1).\n\\]\nThe second conclusion also holds if the finite-quotient vanishing is supplied directly, without the semidirect-product assumptions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The pure-subgroup cochain complex is \\(W\\)-linear and has semilinear \\(Q\\)-action, including conjugation on its arguments:\n\\[\n(\\gamma f)(s_1,\\ldots,s_b)\n=\n\\gamma f(\\gamma^{-1}s_1\\gamma,\\ldots,\\gamma^{-1}s_b\\gamma).\n\\]\nIts differential is \\(W\\)-linear and commutes with this action. Its cohomology is therefore semilinear, whether or not it has separately been shown finite.</p><p>The continuous Hochschild–Serre sequence at discrete coefficients is\n\\[\nH^i\\bigl(Q;H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^b(S_0;M_j)\\bigr)\n\\Longrightarrow H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^{i+b}(G;M_j).\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 kills positive \\(Q\\)-columns, giving\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M_j)\n=\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(S_0;M_j)^Q.\n\\]\nThis proves the finite-quotient bound.</p><p>Continuous cochains into \\(M\\) are compatible cochains into the finite quotients. Their transition maps are degreewise surjective: a continuous cochain has finite value set, and choosing lifts of those values gives a continuous lift. No equivariant section is needed for inhomogeneous cochains.</p><p>The countable inverse-limit sequence is\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\n\\varprojlim\\nolimits_j^1H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^{s-1}(G;M_j)\n\\longrightarrow H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\varprojlim_jH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G;M_j)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nBoth outer terms vanish for \\(s&gt;D+1\\). At the boundary,\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^{D+1}(G;M)\n\\cong\n\\varprojlim\\nolimits_j^1H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^D(G;M_j).\n\\]\nThus the possible extra inverse-limit degree is retained.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Two such resolutions of \\(V\\) have the same stage-\\(s\\) image in \\([W,V]\\) for every object \\(W\\) and every \\(s\\ge0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Equality of relative Adams-stage images — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a stable symmetric monoidal \\(\\infty\\)-category with exact tensor product.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be a unital algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">Call an object \\(E\\)-injective if it is a retract of \\(E\\otimes Z\\) for some \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A relative \\(E\\)-Adams resolution of \\(V\\) consists of triangles\n  \\[\n  T_{s+1}\\longrightarrow T_s\\xrightarrow{q_s}Q_s,\\qquad T_0=V,\n  \\]\n  where every \\(Q_s\\) is \\(E\\)-injective and \\(E\\otimes q_s\\) is split monic.</p><p class=\"li\">Every stage maps to \\(V\\) by its composite down the tower.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Two such resolutions of \\(V\\) have the same stage-\\(s\\) image in \\([W,V]\\) for every object \\(W\\) and every \\(s\\ge0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose\n\\[\nf_s:T_s\\longrightarrow T_s'\n\\]\nover \\(V\\) has been constructed, and put \\(g_s=q_s'f_s\\). Choose a retract\n\\[\nQ_s'\\xrightarrow{\\iota_s}E\\otimes Z_s\n\\xrightarrow{\\rho_s}Q_s',\n\\qquad \\rho_s\\iota_s=1,\n\\]\nand a splitting\n\\[\n\\sigma_s:E\\otimes Q_s\\longrightarrow E\\otimes T_s,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_s(1_E\\otimes q_s)=1.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\bar g_s:\nQ_s\\xrightarrow{\\eta_{Q_s}}E\\otimes Q_s\n\\xrightarrow{\\sigma_s}E\\otimes T_s\n\\xrightarrow{1_E\\otimes(\\iota_sg_s)}E\\otimes E\\otimes Z_s\n\\xrightarrow{\\mu\\otimes1}E\\otimes Z_s\n\\xrightarrow{\\rho_s}Q_s'.\n\\]\nThis extends \\(g_s\\) along \\(q_s\\). Indeed,\n\\[\n\\eta_{Q_s}q_s=(1_E\\otimes q_s)\\eta_{T_s};\n\\]\nthe splitting removes \\(1_E\\otimes q_s\\), and unit naturality followed by the module unit law gives\n\\[\n\\bar g_sq_s\n=\n\\rho_s(\\mu\\otimes1)(1_E\\otimes(\\iota_sg_s))\\eta_{T_s}\n=\n\\rho_s\\iota_sg_s\n=\ng_s.\n\\]</p><p>Complete the square to a map of fiber triangles to obtain\n\\[\nf_{s+1}:T_{s+1}\\to T_{s+1}'.\n\\]\nStarting with \\(f_0=\\mathrm{id}_V\\) gives maps over \\(V\\) at every finite stage, hence one inclusion between stage-\\(s\\) images. Repeat in the other direction for equality.</p><p>The comparison begins at stage zero on \\(V\\), so indices agree. Only finite-stage maps are used; no equivalence of towers is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The strongly convergent descent spectral sequence \\( E_2^{s,q} = H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_a;(E_a)_q) \\Longrightarrow\\pi_{q-s}B_a \\) has the separated homotopy filtration …", "statement": "<p>**The actual Adams filtration — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive height \\(a\\), and work in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_a=\\mathrm{Sp}_{K(a)}\n  \\]\n  with unit \\(B_a=L_{K(a)}S\\) and tensor\n  \\[\n  U\\otimes_aV=L_{K(a)}(U\\wedge V).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  I_a=\\operatorname{fib}(B_a\\to E_a),\\qquad\n  \\epsilon_a:I_a\\to B_a,\\qquad\n  I_a^{\\otimes_a0}=B_a.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The strongly convergent descent spectral sequence\n\\[\nE_2^{s,q}\n=\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_a;(E_a)_q)\n\\Longrightarrow\\pi_{q-s}B_a\n\\]\nhas the separated homotopy filtration\n\\[\nF^s\\pi_*B_a\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\left(\n\\pi_*I_a^{\\otimes_as}\n\\xrightarrow{(\\epsilon_a^{\\otimes_as})_*}\n\\pi_*B_a\n\\right).\n\\]\nIts successive quotients are \\(E_\\infty^{s,*}\\), with the usual total degree, and\n\\[\nF^rF^s\\subseteq F^{r+s}.\n\\]\nThese products are the products of the actual sphere-unit images.</p>", "proof": "<p>Devinatz–Hopkins give the displayed spectral sequence with strong convergence and identify it with the local \\(E_a\\)-Adams spectral sequence [7, Theorem 1(iii)–(iv)]; the full extended group is open in itself. The coefficient topology is the full maximal-ideal-adic topology of [7, Remark 1.3]. Their ideal\n\\[\n(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_{a-1})\n\\]\nagrees degreewise with\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m_a=(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{a-1}),\n\\]\nsince \\(v_i=u_i u^{1-p^i}\\) and \\(u\\) is invertible.</p><p>The tensor powers\n\\[\nT_s=I_a^{\\otimes_as}\n\\]\nform triangles\n\\[\nT_{s+1}\\longrightarrow T_s\n\\xrightarrow{q_s}E_a\\otimes_aT_s.\n\\]\nThe right terms are \\(E_a\\)-injective, and multiplication on \\(E_a\\) retracts \\(E_a\\otimes_aq_s\\). Thus these form a relative Adams resolution. Proposition 8 identifies their stage-\\(s\\) images with those in the Devinatz–Hopkins resolution, with the same index.</p><p>In particular, \\(T_0=B_a\\) gives the full homotopy group, and \\(T_1=I_a\\) gives the kernel of\n\\[\n\\pi_*B_a\\to\\pi_*E_a.\n\\]\nStrong convergence supplies separation and the associated-graded quotients for these actual image groups.</p><p>A map from a suspended sphere to a local object is equivalently a map from the suspended local unit. Tensoring lifts through \\(I_a^{\\otimes_ar}\\) and \\(I_a^{\\otimes_as}\\) gives a lift of their product through \\(I_a^{\\otimes_a(r+s)}\\). This proves multiplicativity.</p><p>Monoidal localization carries the sphere unit and its products to the local unit, so these are the actual sphere-image products. Smashing localization is not needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(x\\in\\pi_dS\\), the unit image of \\(x^k\\) is zero in \\( \\pi_{dk}B_p\\qquad(p\\ge5). \\) For \\(p\\ge7\\), the unit image of the same product is also zero in \\( \\pi_{dk}B_{p+1}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Upper vanishing of the same power — original Proposition 5.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be prime and put\n  \\[\n  d=2p(p-1)-2,\\qquad k=(p-1)^2,\\qquad B_a=L_{K(a)}S.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the filtration of Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(x\\in\\pi_dS\\), the unit image of \\(x^k\\) is zero in\n\\[\n\\pi_{dk}B_p\\qquad(p\\ge5).\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\ge7\\), the unit image of the same product is also zero in\n\\[\n\\pi_{dk}B_{p+1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(a\\) be either upper height in its stated range, and let\n\\[\nx_a\\in\\pi_dB_a\n\\]\nbe the unit image of \\(x\\). Positive stable sphere stems are torsion: suspend into an odd-dimensional sphere in the stable range and apply Serre’s finiteness theorem [28, Chapter V, Section 3, Proposition 3]. Since \\((E_a)_d\\) is torsion-free, \\(x_a\\) maps to zero in \\(\\pi_dE_a\\), so \\(x_a\\in F^1\\).</p><p>Since \\(d\\) is even and \\(E_a\\) has only even homotopy,\n\\[\nF^1\\pi_dB_a/F^2\\pi_dB_a\n\\cong E_\\infty^{1,d+1}=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(x_a\\in F^2\\). Multiplicativity in Proposition 9 gives\n\\[\nx_a^k\\in F^{2k}\\pi_{dk}B_a.\n\\]\nThe numerical margins above the last possible filtration are\n\\[\n2(p-1)^2-(p^2+1)=p^2-4p+1&gt;0\n\\qquad(p\\ge5),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n2(p-1)^2-\\bigl((p+1)^2+1\\bigr)=p(p-6)&gt;0\n\\qquad(p\\ge7).\n\\]\nTherefore \\(2k\\ge a^2+2\\), and Lemma 10.1 gives \\(x_a^k=0\\). By Proposition 9, this is the unit image of the original sphere product \\(x^k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( F^s\\pi_*B_a=0\\qquad(s\\ge a^2+2). \\) This is a statement about homotopy images, not nullity of the corresponding tensor-power map.", "statement": "<p>**The zero upper tail — original Proposition 5.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let either \\(a=p\\) with \\(p\\ge5\\), or \\(a=p+1\\) with \\(p\\ge7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F^s\\pi_*B_a\\) be the actual separated filtration of Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nF^s\\pi_*B_a=0\\qquad(s\\ge a^2+2).\n\\]\nThis is a statement about homotopy images, not nullity of the corresponding tensor-power map.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 7, with \\(V_0=S\\), gives\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_a;(E_a)_q)=0\n\\qquad(s&gt;a^2+1,\\ q\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nAt height \\(p\\), this uses pure-stabilizer dimension and the semilinear Witt trace-one contraction for the order-\\(p\\) Galois quotient. At height \\(p+1\\), the full extended group has no \\(p\\)-torsion. Both retain the possible inverse-limit degree \\(a^2+1\\).</p><p>By Proposition 9, the successive quotients of the actual filtration from \\(a^2+2\\) onward are zero. The tail is constant, and separatedness makes its common value zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(s\\ge0\\), there is a natural equivalence of arrows over \\(\\mathrm{id}_V\\) in \\(\\mathcal C/V\\):", "statement": "<p>**Full finite tensor cubes — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a stable symmetric monoidal \\(\\infty\\)-category whose tensor is exact in each variable.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be an associative unital algebra with unit object \\(\\mathbf1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  J=\\operatorname{fib}(\\mathbf1\\to A),\\qquad\n  \\epsilon:J\\to\\mathbf1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(V\\in\\mathcal C\\), form the augmented Amitsur object\n  \\[\n  V\\longrightarrow C_A^\\bullet(V),\\qquad\n  C_A^q(V)=A^{\\otimes(q+1)}\\otimes V.\n  \\]\n  Cofaces insert the unit and codegeneracies multiply adjacent \\(A\\)-factors.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{Tot}_s\\) be the limit over the full simplex category on \\([0],\\ldots,[s]\\), including codegeneracies.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(s\\ge0\\), there is a natural equivalence of arrows over \\(\\mathrm{id}_V\\) in \\(\\mathcal C/V\\):\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\operatorname{fib}\\bigl(V\\to\\operatorname{Tot}_sC_A^\\bullet(V)\\bigr)\n\\to V\n\\right)\n\\simeq\n\\left(\nJ^{\\otimes(s+1)}\\otimes V\n\\xrightarrow{\\epsilon^{\\otimes(s+1)}\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_V}\nV\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe fiber transition from stage \\(s+1\\) to stage \\(s\\) applies \\(\\epsilon\\) to the final \\(J\\)-factor. Every exact functor \\(\\Phi:\\mathcal C\\to\\mathrm{Sp}\\) preserves this finite fiber sequence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal P_{\\ne\\varnothing}([s])\\) be the poset of nonempty subsets of \\(\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}\\). The functor\n\\[\n\\theta_s:\\mathcal P_{\\ne\\varnothing}([s])\\longrightarrow\\Delta_{\\le s},\n\\qquad\nS\\longmapsto[|S|-1],\n\\]\nuses the order-preserving injection induced by subset inclusion.</p><p>For \\([l]\\), \\(l\\le s\\), the comma category \\(\\theta_s/[l]\\) consists of nonempty subsets equipped with a weakly increasing map to \\([l]\\). It is the poset of nonempty faces of the simplicial complex \\(P(s,l)\\), whose simplices are lists\n\\[\n(i_0,j_0),\\ldots,(i_b,j_b),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n0\\le i_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;i_b\\le s,\\qquad\n0\\le j_0\\le\\cdots\\le j_b\\le l.\n\\]</p><p>This complex is contractible. The base case \\(P(0,0)\\) is a point. Every simplex either omits the first-coordinate value \\(s\\) or contains exactly one vertex \\((s,j)\\). Begin inductively with the cone of vertex \\((s,l)\\) on \\(P(s-1,l)\\). The cone is contractible even when \\(l=s\\), when its base is not covered by induction. For each \\(j&lt;l\\), attach the cone of vertex \\((s,j)\\) along its base \\(P(s-1,j)\\). That base is the exact intersection with the previous complex and is contractible by induction, since \\(j\\le s-1\\). These intersections are simplicial subcomplexes, so each attachment preserves contractibility.</p><p>Thus \\(\\theta_s\\) is homotopy initial. The partial totalization is the limit of the punctured \\((s+1)\\)-cube obtained by tensoring copies of \\(\\mathbf1\\to A\\) and then tensoring with \\(V\\). Its omitted vertex is \\(V\\). Successive fibers and exactness of tensor give\n\\[\nJ^{\\otimes(s+1)}\\otimes V\n\\]\nwith its canonical arrow to \\(V\\). Removing the last cube direction gives the stated transition.</p><p>This is a genuinely finite cubical limit, so an exact \\(\\Phi\\) preserves it. Finiteness of the list of objects in \\(\\Delta_{\\le s}\\) alone would not justify that assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "An \\(A\\)-module has exponent at most one.", "statement": "<p>**Exponent calculus — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C,A,J,\\epsilon\\) be as in Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b\\ge1\\), say that \\(V\\) has \\(A\\)-exponent at most \\(b\\) when the actual map\n  \\[\n  e_b(V)=\\epsilon^{\\otimes b}\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_V:\n  J^{\\otimes b}\\otimes V\\longrightarrow V\n  \\]\n  is null.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. An \\(A\\)-module has exponent at most one.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Exponent bounds are preserved by retracts and by tensoring with arbitrary objects.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For a cofiber sequence \\(U\\to V\\to W\\), bounds \\(b\\) on \\(U\\) and \\(c\\) on \\(W\\) give bound \\(b+c\\) on \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If \\(e_m(V)\\) is null, then \\(V\\) is a retract of an object with \\(m\\) successive layers\n   \\[\n   A\\otimes J^{\\otimes i}\\otimes V,\\qquad 0\\le i&lt;m.\n   \\]</p><p>Consequently, the objects of finite \\(A\\)-exponent form a thick tensor ideal containing \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The unit map of an \\(A\\)-module splits by its action, making the fiber map null. Naturality gives retract invariance, and tensoring a null map gives tensor invariance.</p><p>For the cofiber sequence, the composite\n\\[\nJ^{\\otimes c}\\otimes V\\longrightarrow V\\longrightarrow W\n\\]\nis null, so choose a lift\n\\[\n\\ell:J^{\\otimes c}\\otimes V\\longrightarrow U.\n\\]\nAfter precomposition by \\(b\\) more \\(\\epsilon\\)-factors, naturality identifies the resulting map into \\(U\\) with\n\\[\ne_b(U)(1\\otimes\\ell),\n\\]\nwhich is null. Its composite to \\(V\\) is \\(e_{b+c}(V)\\).</p><p>Finally, the consecutive maps\n\\[\nJ^{\\otimes(i+1)}\\otimes V\\longrightarrow J^{\\otimes i}\\otimes V\n\\]\nhave cofibers \\(A\\otimes J^{\\otimes i}\\otimes V\\). Their composites to \\(V\\) filter \\(\\operatorname{cofib}(e_m(V))\\) by these \\(m\\) layers. Nullity of \\(e_m(V)\\) makes \\(V\\) a retract of that cofiber. The closure assertions give the stated thick tensor ideal.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(b\\ge1\\):", "statement": "<p>**Pages and tested images — original Proposition 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C,A,J,\\epsilon\\) be as in Proposition 11, and let \\(\\Phi:\\mathcal C\\to\\mathrm{Sp}\\) be exact.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(V\\in\\mathcal C\\), and put\n  \\[\n  V'=\\Phi V,\\qquad U_l=\\Phi(J^{\\otimes l}\\otimes V),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  P_s=\\operatorname{Tot}_s\\bigl(\\Phi C_A^\\bullet(V)\\bigr)\\quad(s\\ge0),\n  \\qquad P_s=0\\quad(s&lt;0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Proposition 11 gives compatible fiber sequences\n  \\[\n  U_{s+1}\\longrightarrow V'\\longrightarrow P_s.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the finite-stage augmentation filtration\n  \\[\n  F^s\\pi_dV'\n  =\n  \\ker(\\pi_dV'\\to\\pi_dP_{s-1})\n  =\n  \\operatorname{im}(\\pi_dU_s\\to\\pi_dV').\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the page convention\n  \\[\n  E_2^{s,q}=H^s\\pi_q(\\Phi C_A^\\bullet(V)),\n  \\qquad\n  d_r:(s,q)\\longmapsto(s+r,q+r-1).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(b\\ge1\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(V\\) has \\(A\\)-exponent at most \\(b\\), then\n   \\[\n   E_{b+1}^{s,q}=0\\qquad(s\\ge b).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(V\\) has some finite \\(A\\)-exponent and\n   \\[\n   E_2^{s,q}=0\\qquad(s\\ge b),\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   \\pi_d\\Phi(e_b(V))=0\n   \\]\n   for every \\(d\\).</p><p>The second assertion is zero tested image, not nullity of a map.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use coordinates \\((s,d)\\), where \\(d=q-s\\). The derived couple has\n\\[\nD_r^{s,d}\n=\n\\operatorname{im}(\\pi_dP_{s+r-1}\\to\\pi_dP_s)\n\\]\nand maps\n\\[\ni:D_r^{s,d}\\to D_r^{s-1,d},\\qquad\nj:D_r^{s,d}\\to E_r^{s+r,d-1},\\qquad\nk:E_r^{s,d}\\to D_r^{s,d}.\n\\]\nHere the second \\(E\\)-index is total degree; no convergence assertion is used.</p><p>Let \\(c\\) be an actual exponent bound. Every length-\\(c\\) transition in the fiber tower \\(U_l\\) is null, since it is \\(\\Phi\\) applied to a tensor multiple of \\(e_c(V)\\). For \\(s\\ge0\\), the boundary square is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\pi_dP_{s+c}&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;\\pi_{d-1}U_{s+c+1}\\\\\n\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\,0\\\\\n\\pi_dP_s&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;\\pi_{d-1}U_{s+1}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe image from \\(P_{s+c}\\) has zero boundary and lifts to \\(\\pi_dV'\\); conversely an augmentation class comes from \\(P_{s+c}\\). Hence\n\\[\nD_{c+1}^{s,d}\n=\n\\operatorname{im}(\\pi_dV'\\to\\pi_dP_s).\n\\]\nBoth sides are zero for negative \\(s\\). These image groups have surjective transition \\(i\\).</p><p>The augmentation into \\(P_s\\) is injective on homotopy for \\(s\\ge c-1\\), since its kernel is the image of a map factoring through \\(e_c(V)\\). Thus \\(i\\) is an isomorphism for \\(s\\ge c\\). Exactness of the derived couple gives \\(j=0\\) and, for \\(s\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nE_{c+1}^{s,d}\n\\cong\n\\ker(D_{c+1}^{s,d}\\to D_{c+1}^{s-1,d})\n\\cong\nF^s\\pi_dV'/F^{s+1}\\pi_dV'.\n\\]\nThis is zero for \\(s\\ge c\\). Taking \\(c=b\\) proves part 1.</p><p>For part 2, retain any finite exponent bound \\(c\\). The \\(E_2\\)-line makes the same quotients zero for \\(s\\ge b\\). Since \\(F^c=0\\), descending from \\(c\\), or using monotonicity if \\(c&lt;b\\), gives \\(F^b=0\\). By the definition of the finite-stage filtration, this is exactly\n\\[\n\\pi_d\\Phi(e_b(V))=0.\n\\]</p><p>Only finite cubes are used; the argument never assumes that \\(\\Phi\\) preserves an infinite limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\exp_H(E_t)>m. \\) The obstruction is a class of filtration greater than \\(m\\) surviving to the specified finite page \\(E_{m+1}\\) of the ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence.", "statement": "<p>**Finite-page lower exponent bound — original Proposition 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\) be prime, put\n  \\[\n  t=p-1,\\qquad m=p^2+2,\n  \\]\n  and let \\(E_t\\) be Morava \\(E\\)-theory over \\(k_t=\\mathbb F_{p^t}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  H=C_p\\rtimes C_{t^2}\n  \\]\n  be the finite pure-stabilizer subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathcal D_H=\\operatorname{Fun}(BH,\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)})\\), with\n  \\[\n  A_H=F(H_+,S),\\qquad\n  J_H=\\operatorname{fib}(S\\to A_H),\n  \\]\n  and define \\(\\exp_H\\) using the actual maps\n  \\[\n  J_H^{\\wedge b}\\wedge V\\to V.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\exp_H(E_t)&gt;m.\n\\]\nThe obstruction is a class of filtration greater than \\(m\\) surviving to the specified finite page \\(E_{m+1}\\) of the ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the exact functor \\((-)^{hH}\\), the \\(A_H\\)-Amitsur spectral sequence is the ordinary homotopy fixed point spectral sequence. Its terms are functions on the free \\(H\\)-sets \\(H^{q+1}\\), and taking fixed points gives homogeneous cochains. Proposition 2 gives its cup product and derivation rule without requiring convergence.</p><p>The inequality\n\\[\nm&lt;2t^2+1\n\\]\nholds because the difference is\n\\[\np^2-4p+1&gt;0.\n\\]\nChoose a positive integer \\(N\\) divisible by \\(p\\) with \\(2N&gt;m\\). We show that\n\\[\n\\beta^N\\ne0\n\\]\non \\(E_{m+1}\\), in bidegree \\((2N,2ptN)\\).</p><p>For \\(2\\le r\\le m\\), all possible source and target filtrations are positive, so Lemma 14.1 applies. Even \\(r\\) gives odd internal degree and a zero group. For odd \\(r\\), internal-degree divisibility forces\n\\[\nr=2ta+1,\\qquad 1\\le a&lt;t.\n\\]</p><p>An outgoing target must be\n\\[\n\\alpha\\beta^{N+ta}\\Delta^l.\n\\]\nEquality of internal degrees gives\n\\[\na-1=pt(a+l),\n\\]\nforcing \\(a=1\\).</p><p>An incoming source must be\n\\[\n\\alpha\\beta^{N-ta-1}\\Delta^l.\n\\]\nIts internal-degree equation is\n\\[\na-t=pt(a-l),\n\\]\nwhich is impossible for \\(1\\le a&lt;t\\).</p><p>Thus only the outgoing differential \\(d_{2t+1}\\) remains. The same degree argument shows that \\(\\beta\\) exists up to that page; its only possible incoming differential has length two and odd internal source degree. The derivation rule gives\n\\[\nd_{2t+1}(\\beta^N)\n=\nN\\beta^{N-1}d_{2t+1}(\\beta)\n=\n0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\beta\\) has even total degree, \\(p\\mid N\\), and the target is \\(p\\)-torsion.</p><p>All incoming differentials and all other outgoing possibilities through page \\(m\\) have been excluded. Hence \\(\\beta^N\\) is nonzero on \\(E_{m+1}\\), in filtration \\(2N&gt;m\\). Proposition 13(1) excludes this if \\(\\exp_H(E_t)\\le m\\).</p><p>At \\(p=5\\), \\(m=27\\) and one may take \\(N=15\\); the class has filtration \\(30\\) on \\(E_{28}\\). The proof needs only finite-page survival, not a sphere representative or the abutment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The pure stabilizer \\(S_t\\) contains a finite subgroup \\( H\\cong C_p\\rtimes C_{t^2} \\) for which \\( \\widehat H^*(H;(E_t)_*) = k_t[\\alpha,\\beta^{\\pm1},\\Delta^{\\pm1}]/(\\alpha^2), \\) with …", "statement": "<p>**Pure-stabilizer cohomology — original Theorem 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, relax \\(p\\ge5\\) to any odd prime \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(t=p-1\\), let \\(E_t\\) be Morava \\(E\\)-theory for the height-\\(t\\) Honda formal group over \\(k_t=\\mathbb F_{p^t}\\), and use its coherent stabilizer action.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pure stabilizer \\(S_t\\) contains a finite subgroup\n\\[\nH\\cong C_p\\rtimes C_{t^2}\n\\]\nfor which\n\\[\n\\widehat H^*(H;(E_t)_*)\n=\nk_t[\\alpha,\\beta^{\\pm1},\\Delta^{\\pm1}]/(\\alpha^2),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n|\\alpha|=(1,2t),\\qquad\n|\\beta|=(2,2pt),\\qquad\n|\\Delta|=(0,2pt^2).\n\\]\nThe ordinary-to-Tate comparison is multiplicative and identifies\n\\[\nH^s(H;(E_t)_q)\n\\]\nwith the displayed Tate group for every \\(s&gt;0\\) and every \\(q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The coherent action specializes to the prescribed Honda stabilizer action [7, Theorem 1(ii) and Remark 2.4].</p><p>This is the subgroup denoted \\(F\\) in Barthel–Beaudry–Stojanoska [1, Equation (1.2) and Theorem 4.5], where the height is denoted \\(n\\). The positive-filtration comparison is [1, Remark 2.2]. The pure-group calculation before taking Galois invariants also appears in Heard [10, proof of Proposition 5.5].</p><p>Only the cohomology and its bidegrees are used here; no differential from the Tate spectral sequence is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The actual map \\( e_m(B_h):I_h^{\\otimes_hm}\\longrightarrow B_h \\) is null.", "statement": "<p>**The upper null map and finite layers — original Proposition 9.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\), put \\(h=p\\), and work in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_h=\\mathrm{Sp}_{K(h)}\n  \\]\n  with unit \\(B_h=L_{K(h)}S\\) and tensor \\(\\otimes_h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  I_h=\\operatorname{fib}(B_h\\to E_h),\\qquad\n  m=h^2+2=p^2+2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The actual map\n\\[\ne_m(B_h):I_h^{\\otimes_hm}\\longrightarrow B_h\n\\]\nis null. Thus\n\\[\n\\exp_{E_h}(B_h)\\le m,\n\\]\nand \\(B_h\\) is a retract of an object with \\(m\\) successive layers\n\\[\nE_h\\otimes_hI_h^{\\otimes_hi},\n\\qquad 0\\le i&lt;m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First obtain some finite exponent for the actual local unit, independently of all tests. Devinatz–Hopkins prove that every \\(K(h)\\)-local spectrum belongs to the thick tensor ideal generated by \\(E_h\\) in \\(\\mathcal C_h\\) [7, Appendix A, Proposition A.3]. Its descendability form is Mathew [20, Proposition 10.10].</p><p>By Proposition 12, finite-\\(E_h\\)-exponent objects form a thick tensor ideal containing \\(E_h\\). Hence \\(B_h\\) has some finite exponent \\(c\\). This is a bound on an actual map before any finite test is chosen.</p><p>Apply the full local unit Amitsur construction to \\(B_h\\), then the exact ordinary functor \\(F(V_0,-)\\) for finite ordinary \\(V_0\\). Proposition 6 identifies the second page as\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s\\bigl(G_h;\\pi_q(E_h\\wedge DV_0)\\bigr).\n\\]\nProposition 7 makes it zero for\n\\[\ns\\ge h^2+2=m.\n\\]\nUsing the already known finite exponent \\(c\\), Proposition 13(2) gives\n\\[\n[V_0,I_h^{\\otimes_hm}]\n\\longrightarrow\n[V_0,B_h]\n\\]\nequal to zero for every finite ordinary \\(V_0\\).</p><p>Thus \\(e_m(B_h)\\), regarded as an ordinary map, vanishes after every finite ordinary precomposition. Lemma 15.1 makes it null in ordinary spectra and hence null in the full subcategory \\(\\mathcal C_h\\).</p><p>For the finite layers, set \\(I_h^{\\otimes_h0}=B_h\\), and for \\(0\\le j\\le m\\) put\n\\[\nF_j=\n\\operatorname{cofib}\n\\left(I_h^{\\otimes_hm}\\longrightarrow\nI_h^{\\otimes_h(m-j)}\\right).\n\\]\nThese form a finite filtration\n\\[\n0=F_0\\longrightarrow F_1\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow F_m.\n\\]\nThe cofiber comparison for consecutive composites gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cofib}(F_j\\to F_{j+1})\n\\simeq\nE_h\\otimes_hI_h^{\\otimes_h(m-j-1)}\n\\quad(0\\le j&lt;m).\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nF_m=\\operatorname{cofib}(e_m(B_h)).\n\\]\nA nullhomotopy of \\(e_m(B_h)\\) makes \\(B_h\\) a retract of this cofiber.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If the precomposition of \\(f\\) with every map from a finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum to \\(U\\) is null, then \\(f\\) is null.", "statement": "<p>**Ordinary phantoms into a local sphere — original Lemma 9.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In place of the fixed height \\(h=p\\), let \\(a\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B_a=L_{K(a)}S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:U\\to B_a\\) be an ordinary map of \\(p\\)-local spectra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If the precomposition of \\(f\\) with every map from a finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectrum to \\(U\\) is null, then \\(f\\) is null.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nM_aS=\\operatorname{fib}(L_aS\\to L_{a-1}S),\n\\]\nand let \\(I_{\\mathrm{BC}}\\) be the ordinary Brown–Comenetz dualizing spectrum for the injective group \\(\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\). Gross–Hopkins duality says that\n\\[\nD_a=F(M_aS,I_{\\mathrm{BC}})\n\\]\nis \\(K(a)\\)-local and invertible for \\(\\otimes_a\\) [12, Theorem 6; 15, Theorem 10.2(b),(e)]. The identification with this monochromatic object uses [15, Theorem 6.19].</p><p>For local \\(Z\\), the ordinary spectrum \\(F(R,Z)\\) is local: a map into it from a \\(K(a)\\)-acyclic \\(V\\) corresponds to a map\n\\[\nV\\wedge R\\to Z,\n\\]\nand \\(V\\wedge R\\) remains acyclic. Thus ordinary function spectra compute the local internal Hom [15, Theorem 7.1].</p><p>Invertibility of \\(D_a\\), followed by ordinary closed adjunction, gives\n\\[\nB_a\\simeq F(D_a,D_a)\n\\simeq F(D_a\\wedge M_aS,I_{\\mathrm{BC}}).\n\\]\nThe displayed smash is ordinary. Set \\(C=D_a\\wedge M_aS\\). Brown–Comenetz representability gives, naturally in \\(U\\),\n\\[\n[U,B_a]\n\\cong\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb Z}\n\\bigl(\\pi_0(U\\wedge C),\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(U\\) as a filtered homotopy colimit of finite ordinary \\(p\\)-local spectra \\(U_i\\). Ordinary smash and homotopy groups preserve this colimit, so\n\\[\n\\pi_0(U\\wedge C)=\\varinjlim_i\\pi_0(U_i\\wedge C).\n\\]\nThe functional corresponding to \\(f\\) vanishes on every term by hypothesis. It vanishes on the colimit, so \\(f=0\\).</p><p>This uses ordinary finite tests and does not interchange \\(K(a)\\)-localization with the filtered colimit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an underlying class \\(z\\in\\pi_0D'\\) whose \\(H\\)-trace \\( \\sum_{g\\in H}g(z) \\) is a unit.", "statement": "<p>**The completed mixed trace — original Proposition 10.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\), put \\(t=p-1\\) and \\(h=p\\), and let\n  \\[\n  H=C_p\\rtimes C_{t^2}\n  \\]\n  be the finite pure-stabilizer subgroup acting on \\(E_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathcal D_H=\\operatorname{Fun}(BH,\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  D=E_t\\wedge E_h,\\qquad D'=L_{K(t)}D,\n  \\]\n  with \\(H\\) acting on the first factor.</p><p class=\"li\">Monoidal localization into \\(\\mathcal C_t\\), followed by the lax monoidal inclusion into ordinary spectra, gives \\(D'\\) a commutative algebra structure in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\). The map \\(D\\to D'\\) is equivariant and a map of these algebras.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\exp_H\\) be defined using \\(A_H=F(H_+,S)\\) and \\(J_H=\\operatorname{fib}(S\\to A_H)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an underlying class \\(z\\in\\pi_0D'\\) whose \\(H\\)-trace\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in H}g(z)\n\\]\nis a unit. Every \\(D'\\)-module in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\) therefore has \\(H\\)-exponent at most one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 16.2 and 16.3 give\n\\[\n\\bar z\\in R,\\qquad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\bar z)=1.\n\\]\nLift it to \\(z_0\\in\\pi_0D\\), and put\n\\[\nv=\\sum_{g\\in H}g(z_0).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nv-1\\in\\mathfrak m_t\\pi_0D.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 4 makes \\(\\pi_*D\\) flat over \\((E_t)_*\\). Proposition 5 identifies the homotopy of the actual map \\(D\\to D'\\) with ordinary \\(\\mathfrak m_t\\)-adic completion.</p><p>At level \\(\\pi_0D/\\mathfrak m_t^j\\pi_0D\\), define\n\\[\nw_j=\\sum_{k=0}^{j-1}(1-v)^k.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nvw_j=1-(1-v)^j=1.\n\\]\nThe inverses are compatible under reduction. Here \\(j\\) is a finite truncation level; the quotient rings need not be finite sets.</p><p>The comparison with completion is induced by the actual ring map \\(D\\to D'\\). Multiplication by the image of \\(v\\) is \\(\\mathcal O_t\\)-linear and adically continuous and agrees with ordinary multiplication on the dense image. It therefore agrees with levelwise multiplication on the completion. The compatible \\(w_j\\) give an inverse to the image of \\(v\\) in \\(\\pi_0D'\\).</p><p>Localization is equivariant, so this image is the trace of the image of \\(z_0\\). Lemma 16.1 now gives exponent at most one for every \\(D'\\)-module.</p><p>If the completed ring is zero, its unit is zero and every unital module is contractible, giving the same conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The unit map \\( j:U\\longrightarrow A_H\\wedge U \\) has a coherent \\(H\\)-equivariant retraction.", "statement": "<p>**Coherent trace splitting — original Lemma 10.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">More generally than Proposition 16, let \\(H\\) be any finite group and let \\(D'\\) be a commutative algebra in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_H=\\operatorname{Fun}(BH,\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose an underlying class \\(z\\in\\pi_0D'\\) has unit trace\n  \\[\n  v=\\sum_{g\\in H}g(z)\\in(\\pi_0D')^\\times.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_H=F(H_+,S)\\), and let \\(U\\) be a \\(D'\\)-module in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The unit map\n\\[\nj:U\\longrightarrow A_H\\wedge U\n\\]\nhas a coherent \\(H\\)-equivariant retraction. Hence \\(\\exp_H(U)\\le1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(H\\) is finite,\n\\[\nA_H\\wedge U\\simeq F(H_+,U)\n\\]\nwith diagonal action. Untwisting the value at \\(g\\) by \\(g^{-1}\\) identifies this with coinduction:\n\\[\n\\theta:A_H\\wedge U\n\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}\n\\operatorname{Coind}_1^H(\\operatorname{Res}_1^HU).\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n(hf)(g)=h f(h^{-1}g),\\qquad\n(\\theta f)(g)=g^{-1}f(g),\n\\]\nand the latter turns the action into translation\n\\[\n(h\\theta f)(g)=(\\theta f)(h^{-1}g).\n\\]</p><p>For finite \\(H\\), the finite coproduct-product equivalence identifies induction with coinduction. Induction adjunction takes underlying multiplication\n\\[\n\\mu_z:U\\to U\n\\]\nto a coherent \\(H\\)-map\n\\[\nr_z:A_H\\wedge U\\to U.\n\\]\nUnder \\(\\theta\\), the component of \\(j\\) at \\(g\\) is \\(g^{-1}:U\\to U\\). The induction map has component \\(g\\mu_z\\), so the underlying composite is\n\\[\nr_zj\n=\n\\sum_{g\\in H}g\\mu_zg^{-1}\n=\n\\mu_{\\sum_gg(z)}\n=\n\\mu_v.\n\\]\nThe middle equality uses compatibility of the module structure and the action.</p><p>Since \\(v\\) is a unit, \\(r_zj\\) is an underlying equivalence. Equivalences of \\(H\\)-diagrams are detected on underlying objects, so\n\\[\n(r_zj)^{-1}r_z\n\\]\nretracts \\(j\\) in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\). Its fiber map is null.</p><p>Only an underlying class \\(z\\) was used; no homotopy-fixed lift was needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(g\\in H\\) satisfies \\(x\\circ g=x\\), then \\(g=1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Freeness on field-valued points — original Lemma 10.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to \\(p,t,h,H,D\\) from Proposition 16.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  R=\\pi_0D/\\mathfrak m_t\\pi_0D.\n  \\]\n  The ideal is \\(H\\)-stable.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x:R\\to\\Omega\\) be a unital map to a field.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(g\\in H\\) satisfies \\(x\\circ g=x\\), then \\(g=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a complex-oriented even periodic commutative ring spectrum \\(B\\), an orientation and a periodic unit give a degree-zero coordinate. The finite projective-space calculation and its surjective restriction tower give\n\\[\nB^0(\\mathbb CP^\\infty)=(\\pi_0B)[[z_B]].\n\\]\nProducts give the formal group law. A unital ring-spectrum map \\(f:B\\to C\\) carries the orientation to an orientation and the periodic unit to a unit, so its coordinate has invertible linear coefficient in the coordinate of \\(C\\). The inverse coordinate substitution gives an isomorphism from the base change of the formal group of \\(B\\) to that of \\(C\\). This respects group laws and composites. Subsequent base changes are algebraic base changes along coefficient-ring maps.</p><p>By Proposition 4, \\(D\\) is even periodic, with orientation and periodic unit from \\(E_t\\). Let\n\\[\n\\rho:\\pi_0D\\to\\Omega\n\\]\nbe the map through \\(x\\). Write\n\\[\n\\iota_t:E_t\\to D,\\qquad \\iota_h:E_h\\to D\n\\]\nfor the factor maps, and \\(\\rho_t,\\rho_h\\) for the resulting coefficient maps. The first factors through\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_t/\\mathfrak m_t=k_t\\longrightarrow\\Omega.\n\\]\nThe last map is injective because it is a unital map from a field.</p><p>After base change, the factor maps give formal-group isomorphisms\n\\[\n\\phi_t:F_{t,\\Omega}\\xrightarrow{\\cong}F_{D,\\Omega},\n\\qquad\n\\phi_h:F_{h,\\Omega}\\xrightarrow{\\cong}F_{D,\\Omega}.\n\\]\nThe equality \\(x\\circ g=x\\) gives\n\\[\n\\rho\\circ\\pi_0(g_D)=\\rho,\n\\]\nso naturality makes \\(g_D\\) an automorphism \\(u_g\\) of the single formal group \\(F_{D,\\Omega}\\).</p><p>Since \\(g_D\\iota_h=\\iota_h\\),\n\\[\nu_g\\phi_h=\\phi_h,\n\\]\nhence \\(u_g\\) is the identity. Since \\(g_D\\iota_t=\\iota_tg\\),\n\\[\nu_g\\phi_t=\\phi_tg_\\Omega,\n\\]\nso \\(g_\\Omega\\) is the identity.</p><p>On the special fiber, the pure stabilizer element \\(g\\) is its specified automorphism of the Honda formal group over \\(k_t\\), by Lubin–Tate deformation [19, Proposition 3.3 and paragraph 3.4] and its coherent realization. If \\(g\\ne1\\), some coefficient of \\(g(X)-X\\) is nonzero in \\(k_t\\). It remains nonzero under the injection \\(k_t\\to\\Omega\\), a contradiction.</p><p>The map to \\(\\Omega\\) is used only for algebraic base change; no ring-spectrum map to a spectrum with coefficient field \\(\\Omega\\) is required. Equality of actual field-valued maps, not merely invariance of kernels, is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( 1\\in\\operatorname{im}(\\operatorname{Tr}). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Algebraic trace one — original Lemma 10.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Independently of the spectral setting, let a finite group \\(H\\) act on a commutative ring \\(R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that for every unital map \\(x:R\\to\\Omega\\) to a field,\n  \\[\n  x\\circ g=x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad g=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Tr}:R\\to R^H,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{Tr}(r)=\\sum_{g\\in H}g(r).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n1\\in\\operatorname{im}(\\operatorname{Tr}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The trace image is an ideal of \\(R^H\\), since trace is additive and\n\\[\na\\operatorname{Tr}(r)=\\operatorname{Tr}(ar)\n\\qquad(a\\in R^H).\n\\]\nSuppose it is proper and choose a maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak q\\) containing it. For every \\(r\\in R\\), the monic orbit polynomial\n\\[\n\\prod_{g\\in H}(X-g(r))\n\\]\nhas coefficients in \\(R^H\\), so \\(R\\) is integral over \\(R^H\\). Lying over gives a prime \\(\\mathfrak p\\subset R\\) above \\(\\mathfrak q\\). The quotient and inclusion into the fraction field give\n\\[\nx:R\\longrightarrow\\Omega=\\operatorname{Frac}(R/\\mathfrak p)\n\\]\nkilling \\(\\mathfrak q\\).</p><p>The multiplicative unital maps\n\\[\n\\chi_g:R\\to\\Omega,\\qquad \\chi_g(r)=x(g(r)),\n\\]\nare pairwise distinct by hypothesis. Distinct such maps are linearly independent as functions. Indeed, suppose\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^v a_i\\chi_i=0\n\\]\nis a nonzero relation with the least possible number of nonzero coefficients. One term is impossible by evaluation at \\(1\\). If \\(v&gt;1\\), choose \\(b\\in R\\) distinguishing \\(\\chi_1\\) from \\(\\chi_v\\). Subtract \\(\\chi_v(b)\\) times the relation at \\(r\\) from the relation at \\(br\\), obtaining\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;v}a_i\\bigl(\\chi_i(b)-\\chi_v(b)\\bigr)\\chi_i(r)=0.\n\\]\nThe coefficient at \\(i=1\\) is nonzero, contradicting minimality.</p><p>In particular, \\(\\sum_g\\chi_g\\) is not zero: every coefficient is \\(1\\), even if the characteristic divides \\(|H|\\). But this sum equals \\(x\\circ\\operatorname{Tr}\\), which is zero since \\(x\\) kills the trace ideal. This contradiction proves the result.</p><p>If \\(R\\) is the zero ring, the conclusion holds directly with \\(1=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The cross-height upper exponent satisfies \\( \\exp_H\\bigl(T(B_h)\\bigr)\\le m. \\) A homotopy retraction of \\(i_{h,X}\\) would exhibit \\(E_t\\) as a retract of \\(T(B_h)\\) in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\).", "statement": "<p>**Transport of finite layers and comparison with the canonical unit — original Proposition 11.1 and the comparison in Corollary 11.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\ge5\\), put\n  \\[\n  t=p-1,\\qquad h=p,\\qquad m=p^2+2,\n  \\]\n  and let \\(H=C_p\\rtimes C_{t^2}\\) be the pure-stabilizer subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B_h=L_{K(h)}S,\\qquad\n  T:\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)}\\to\\mathcal D_H,\\qquad\n  T(V)=L_{K(t)}(E_t\\wedge V),\n  \\]\n  with \\(H\\) acting on \\(E_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X=S_p^\\wedge\\), and define\n  \\[\n  i_{h,X}=L_t(\\eta_X^{(h)}):\n  L_tX\\longrightarrow L_tL_{K(h)}X.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The cross-height upper exponent satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\exp_H\\bigl(T(B_h)\\bigr)\\le m.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A homotopy retraction of \\(i_{h,X}\\) would exhibit \\(E_t\\) as a retract of \\(T(B_h)\\) in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For part 1, the inclusion\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_h\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{Sp}_{(p)}\n\\]\nis exact and lax symmetric monoidal. Since \\(E_h\\) is local, the localization unit identifies the included local algebra \\(E_h\\) with its ordinary algebra. Each local layer\n\\[\nE_h\\otimes_hI_h^{\\otimes_hi}\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 15 therefore has an ordinary \\(E_h\\)-module structure.</p><p>Ordinary smash with \\(E_t\\) makes the layer a module over\n\\[\nD=E_t\\wedge E_h\n\\]\nwith compatible \\(H\\)-action. The \\(K(t)\\)-acyclic spectra form a smash ideal. Thus localization into \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) is symmetric monoidal for its localized tensor, and inclusion into ordinary spectra is lax symmetric monoidal. Localization gives the transformed layer a\n\\[\nD'=L_{K(t)}D\n\\]\nmodule structure in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\). Proposition 16 gives every transformed layer \\(H\\)-exponent at most one.</p><p>The functor \\(T\\) preserves cofiber sequences and retracts. Apply it to the finite filtration and retraction of Proposition 15, then add the \\(m\\) layer bounds using Proposition 12. This gives\n\\[\n\\exp_H(T(B_h))\\le m.\n\\]\nOnly ordinary module structures on the individual layers are used; no preservation by \\(T\\) of the \\(\\mathcal C_h\\)-tensor product is required.</p><p>For part 2, \\(T\\) inverts \\(K(t)\\)-equivalences: the cofiber of such a map is \\(K(t)\\)-acyclic and remains so after ordinary smash with \\(E_t\\). Every \\(L_t\\)-localization unit is a \\(K(t)\\)-equivalence. Proposition 1 says that the actual completion map\n\\[\nc:S\\to X\n\\]\nis a \\(K(a)\\)-equivalence for every positive \\(a\\), in particular for \\(a=t,h\\).</p><p>Write \\(\\lambda_Z:Z\\to L_tZ\\). Naturality gives the commuting square\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\nS&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\eta_S^{(h)}}&amp;B_h\\\\\n\\downarrow{\\lambda_Xc}&amp;&amp;\n\\downarrow{\\lambda_{L_{K(h)}X}\\,L_{K(h)}(c)}\\\\\nL_tX&amp;\\xrightarrow{i_{h,X}}&amp;L_tL_{K(h)}X.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nBoth vertical maps become equivalences under \\(T\\). On the left, use the completion and lower-localization \\(K(t)\\)-equivalences. On the right, \\(L_{K(h)}(c)\\) is an equivalence, followed by a \\(K(t)\\)-equivalence.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nT(S)\\simeq E_t\n\\]\nwith coherent \\(H\\)-action, since \\(E_t\\) is \\(K(t)\\)-local. A homotopy retraction of \\(i_{h,X}\\), after applying \\(T\\) and these identifications, would therefore exhibit \\(E_t\\) as a retract of \\(T(B_h)\\) in \\(\\mathcal D_H\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [4, 32, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 24, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 28, "cite"], [10, 30, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 28, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 26, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [14, 26, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 1, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 3, "cite"], [22, 26, "cite"], [22, 32, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 3, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [26, 32, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 32, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [30, 28, "cite"], [31, 28, "cite"], [32, 3, "cite"]]});
